<h2>6. Your commitments</h2>
<ul>
  <li>You alone are responsible for the content and files you upload, and you declare that you have the right to use them and share them with your client.</li>
  <li>You inform your clients that their data (name, email, approvals) is processed through Signoff, and you comply with the GDPR towards them; see the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.</li>
  <li>It is forbidden to upload content that is unlawful, infringes the rights of others, is malicious (viruses) or is meant to deceive; to use the service for spam; to try to access other people's data; to disrupt the service; to bypass the technical limits.</li>
</ul>

<h2>6a. Technical limits</h2>
<ul>
  <li><%= uploadRules.size %> MB maximum per file, <%= uploadRules.count %> files maximum per milestone or delivery, <%= uploadRules.quota %> MB maximum per account.</li>
  <li>Accepted formats: <%= uploadRules.types %>. The type is recognised from the content of the file, not from its name; other formats (SVG, HTML, archives, executables…) are refused.</li>
</ul>

<h2>7. How approvals work</h2>
<ul>
  <li>An approved milestone is locked: its content can no longer be changed. Any change goes through a new milestone or a supplement. The freelancer can still delete it, with its history.</li>
  <li>Approval is a deliberate action by the client on the page sent to them: they type their full name and confirm. The link stays usable for 7 days after approval.</li>
  <li>Signoff never approves a milestone automatically when the client stays silent. When the project sets a validation deadline, the reminders sent to the client (halfway through and the day before the deadline) are mere notifications; once the deadline has passed, the history says so and the freelancer is told, but the milestone keeps waiting for the client's own decision.</li>
  <li>Depending on the activity chosen in the profile (video, design, illustration, photography), the app says "delivery"; for other activities, "milestone". In these terms, both mean the same thing.</li>
  <li>The freelancer alone is responsible for who receives the link and for sending it correctly. Anyone who has the link can act as the client: only share it with them.</li>
</ul>

<h2>8. Intellectual property</h2>
<p>The service, its code, its design and its brand remain the property of the publisher. You keep all rights to your content; you only grant us the right to host it and show it to your client in order to provide the service.</p>

<h2>9. Liability</h2>
<p>To the extent permitted by law, and except in case of fraud, gross negligence or intentional misconduct by the publisher, the publisher's liability is limited to direct damage and, for all damage together, to the amount paid by the user for the service over the <%- legal('liabilityMonths') %> before the event giving rise to the damage (zero during the free beta). The publisher is not liable for indirect loss (loss of profit, customers, data or opportunities), nor for disputes between a freelancer and their client. These limits do not apply where the law prohibits limiting liability.</p>

<h2>10. Personal data</h2>
<p>How data is processed is described in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>, which forms part of these terms. For your clients' data, Signoff acts as a processor under the agreement in section 15 below.</p>

<h2>11. Suspension and termination</h2>
<p>You can delete your account at any time from "My profile", with the effects described in section 4. The publisher may suspend or delete an account that breaches these terms or is misused, or end the service with <%- legal('serviceEndNoticeDays') %> notice so that you can retrieve your data (a copy on request, certificates to print or save as PDF).</p>

<h2>12. Pricing and payment</h2>
<p>No payment is requested at this time. Plans, prices and the terms of billing, cancellation and refunds will be added here before any switch to a paid service.</p>

<h2>13. Changes to the terms</h2>
<p>The publisher may change these terms; significant changes are announced <%- legal('termsChangeNoticeDays') %> in advance by email or in the app. Continuing to use the service after they take effect means you accept them.</p>

<h2>14. Governing law and disputes</h2>
<p>These terms are governed by Belgian law. Unless a mandatory rule provides otherwise, the courts of <%- legal('courtCity') %> have jurisdiction. Before taking any action, the parties seek an amicable solution by writing to <%- legal('contactEmail') %>.</p>

<h2 id="dpa">15. Data processing agreement (Article 28 GDPR)</h2>
<p>This section is the processing contract required by Article 28(3) of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 ("GDPR"). It applies to the personal data that the user (the "controller") entrusts to Signoff (the "processor") for their clients.</p>

<h3>15.1 Subject matter, duration, nature and purpose</h3>
<p>The processor hosts and processes the data needed to send milestones or deliveries to the controller's clients for approval, collect their approval or change requests, and produce approval certificates. Processing lasts as long as the user's account, plus the periods set out in 15.9.</p>

<h3>15.2 Data and data subjects</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Data subjects</strong>: the controller's clients and their representatives or employees, and any people mentioned in the deliverables.</li>
  <li><strong>Categories of data</strong>: name, email address, full name typed on approval, comments (including file references), IP address, browser (user agent), date and time of actions, content of deliverables and files.</li>
  <li>No special categories of data (Art. 9 GDPR) are expected. The controller undertakes not to upload any in the deliverables.</li>
</ul>

<h3>15.3 Instructions</h3>
<p>The processor processes the data only on documented instructions from the controller. These terms and the use of the service's features are those instructions. The processor immediately informs the controller if, in its opinion, an instruction infringes the GDPR or other data protection rules, or if Union or Member State law requires it to process data, unless that law prohibits such information.</p>

<h3>15.4 Confidentiality</h3>
<p>People authorised to process the data are bound by confidentiality.</p>

<h3>15.5 Security</h3>
<p>The processor implements the technical and organisational measures required by Article 32 GDPR, in particular: encrypted connections (HTTPS), passwords stored as hashes, access control limiting each user to their own projects, protection against forged forms, rate limiting, database lock on approved milestones, backups every night.</p>

<h3>15.6 Sub-processors</h3>
<p>The controller gives general authorisation for the sub-processors listed in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> (Render, Neon, Cloudflare, GitHub, Resend). The processor informs the controller by email of any addition or replacement at least 30 days in advance; the controller may object on reasonable data protection grounds and, failing agreement, delete their account. The processor imposes equivalent data protection obligations on each sub-processor by contract and remains responsible for their performance.</p>

<h3>15.7 Transfers outside the European Economic Area</h3>
<p>Any transfer of data outside the EEA relies on an adequacy decision (including the EU–US Data Privacy Framework for certified providers) or on the European Commission's standard contractual clauses.</p>

<h3>15.8 Assistance</h3>
<p>Taking into account the nature of the processing, the processor assists the controller with appropriate measures: in responding to requests from data subjects exercising their rights (forwarding without delay any request received directly); in ensuring the security of processing; and, where applicable, in carrying out a data protection impact assessment and prior consultation of the supervisory authority.</p>
<p>In the event of a personal data breach, the processor notifies the controller without undue delay and no later than <strong>48 hours</strong> after becoming aware of it, with the information available to allow the controller to meet its own obligations (Articles 33 and 34 GDPR).</p>

<h3>15.9 End of processing</h3>
<p>Before deleting their account, the controller can ask for a copy of their data. Deleting the account erases the data from the database at once; files in storage are erased right after or, failing that, within <%- legal('storageCleanupDays') %>; backups holding a copy expire after <%- legal('backupRetentionDays') %>.</p>

<h3>15.10 Audits and information</h3>
<p>The processor makes available to the controller the information needed to demonstrate compliance with Article 28 GDPR, and allows audits, including inspections, by the controller or an auditor it appoints, with reasonable notice and a confidentiality undertaking.</p>

<h3>15.11 Controller's obligations</h3>
<p>The controller declares that it has a legal basis for entrusting this data to Signoff, and informs its clients of the processing. The approval page shows the controller's clients information about the data recorded on approval.</p>
